From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: seedds <seedds@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 16:23:09 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] The samples-passed counter is one running total in queue
 order: a game that never resets it reads its occlusion queries again (Red
 Dead Redemption's distant characters had no bodies), and a report a
 submission after its reset no longer reads slots a later submission overwrote
 (PR #1867)

On the GPU the counter runs from the queue's creation and ResetCounter zeroes it. The host
counted only from a reset, so a game that never resets read 0 from every report: Red Dead
Redemption's character occlusion queries (end - begin of two reports around each box) counted
every character as hidden and dropped the bodies of those a few metres away.

Each report also re-read every visibility slot since the reset, including slots of earlier
submissions whose buffer the ring had already handed to a later one: a reset and a draw in one
submission and a half-sized draw and the report in the next read 64 samples instead of 96.

Now post_run keeps one running total in submission order under g_post_mu. A report adds the
slots since the previous report and writes the total; the slots left at a submission's end are
added at its completion, so each slot is read once while its buffer is still that
submission's; a reset zeroes the total at its place in the queue. Counting starts at the first
reset or, with none before it, at the first report.

Checked: t_render_enable_queue's two new checks (end - begin with no reset across submissions;
a reset with its report one submission later) fail on main and pass; the whole gfx run list
passes (149 groups); test_docs. The contributor's Red Dead Redemption 1.0.4 replay and play on
an M4 were on the PR's first version, which gives that game the same values. No regression
run: no local game has libraries for chain dedc9391c30b, and translating one is far over the
five minutes a game. Uncertain reach (every occlusion-query game's path).

Co-authored-by: Robert McFadden <20628004+robandstuff@users.noreply.github.com>

Upstream-Status: backport cfbdd93ca5cab4be6e7dfd106d72e6a3bdeed262
Reason: Red Dead Redemption's distant characters lost their bodies: upstream's fix (seedds/nxnative#1867, landed as cfbdd93c) until the pin passes it
Issue: LNPANDY/NX2Apple#999

