+### Samples-passed reports with no reset before them
+
+- **Symptom:** characters a few metres away drawn as their hats and props only, their bodies missing from the main view while their shadows are there; near characters draw fully. A log line or a probe shows every samples-passed report reading 0.
+- **Cause:** the host started counting samples passed only at `nvnCommandBufferResetCounter(SAMPLES_PASSED)`, and a report before any reset wrote 0. On the GPU the counter runs from the queue's creation, so a game that never resets it reads end − begin of two reports around each query, and gets the samples between them. Red Dead Redemption never resets it: every query read 0, every character counted as hidden.
+- **Fix:** in `nvn_mtl.m` (Occlusion queries), for every game, one model of the counter: a running total that `post_run` keeps in submission order under `g_post_mu`. A report hands the visibility slots since the previous report to its submission's completion, which adds them to the total and writes it; the slots left at a submission's end are added at its completion (so each slot is read once, while its buffer still belongs to that submission); a reset drops the slots before it and zeroes the total at that point in the queue. Counting starts at the first reset or, with none before it, at the first report (the pass is split there if its encoder had no visibility buffer); the log says `occlusion queries: samples passed reported with no ResetCounter before it` once. Games that reset read the same values as before; each report used to sum every slot since the reset, reading slots of earlier submissions whose visibility buffer the ring could already have handed to a later one: in `t_render_enable_queue`, a reset and a draw in one submission and a half-sized draw and the report in the next read 64 samples instead of 96, the second draw's count twice. The test also checks end − begin across submissions with no reset.
+- **Check:** on the change's first version (a separate mode for games that never reset; the same values for them), Red Dead Redemption 1.0.4 (Apple M4, macOS 27.0.1), a recorded replay out of the saloon in Armadillo: the distant characters have their bodies at frames 2,280–2,340, frame time unchanged at 33.3 ms, no `[RED]`; played by hand, the same.
+- **Found on:** Red Dead Redemption (`01007820196A6000`): `rage::CharacterVisQueries::TestVisiblity` takes each character's result from `GetOcclusionQueryData`, end − begin of two reports around its box (about 35 queries and 324 reports a frame, no reset), and puts a character at or below its threshold in the occluded list, which `renDrawBucketContainer::AddToDrawList` uses to drop its low-detail body.
+
 ## Vulkan layer
 
 The Vulkan layer (`dm/gfx/vk/hle_vk.m`) was written for Hades; Super Mario Galaxy needed the additions below, each found from a red line or a screenshot on the route to the Star Festival.
